    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d441">

                <pb facs="0641" n="541" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg541"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.51 War Credits/673</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >MacVeagh</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 104</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-07-13">July 13, 1934</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I enclose a copy of a memorandum<anchor
                        n="9" xml:id="d441-f1"/> regarding the payment to this Government on July 6,
                    1934 of $196,128 by the Government of <pb facs="0642" n="542" xml:id="pg542"/>
                    Greece. As explained in this memorandum the sum in question is understood to
                    represent 27½ per cent of the interest payments due May 10 and November 10,
                    1933, and 35 per cent of the interest payment due on May 10, 1934, on the “new
                    loan” provided for in Part II of the American-Hellenic Debt Funding Agreement of
                    May 10, 1929.</p>
                <p>The Department would be interested in receiving your comments on this payment,
                    particularly any information as to whether the necessary funds were released by
                    the International Financial Commission and, if so, whether the release met with
                    the approval of the French member of the Commission.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William
                            Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
